  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "For $a,b\\geq0$, prove that $ab\\leq\\left(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2-ab=\\dfrac{(a-b)^2}{4}\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2-ab&=\\frac{a^2+2ab+b^2}{4}-ab\\\\\\left(\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2-ab&=\\frac{a^2-2ab+b^2}{4}\\\\\\left(\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2-ab&=\\frac{(a-b)^2}{4}\\\\\\left(\\frac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2-ab&\\geq0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $ab\\leq\\left(\\dfrac{a+b}{2}\\right)^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that $$(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)\\leq\\left(\\frac{\\log_b(cd)}{2}\\right)^2.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(\\dfrac{\\log_b(cd)}{2}\\right)^2-(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)=\\dfrac{(\\log_b c-\\log_b d)^2}{4}\\geq0$.\n\nUse the product law and the squared-difference identity from part (a).",
          "worked": "The squared-difference identity in part (a) holds for any real numbers. Using $\\log_b(cd)=\\log_b c+\\log_b d$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac{\\log_b(cd)}{2}\\right)^2-(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)&=\\left(\\frac{\\log_b c+\\log_b d}{2}\\right)^2-(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)\\\\\\left(\\frac{\\log_b(cd)}{2}\\right)^2-(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)&=\\frac{(\\log_b c-\\log_b d)^2}{4}\\\\\\left(\\frac{\\log_b(cd)}{2}\\right)^2-(\\log_b c)(\\log_b d)&\\geq0.\\end{align*}$$\nThis proves the required inequality.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)<9$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)<\\left(\\dfrac{\\log_2 63}{2}\\right)^2<9$.\n\nApply part (b), then use $63<64$ and $\\log_2 64=6$.",
          "worked": "Since $\\log_2 7\\ne\\log_2 9$, the squared difference in part (b) is positive. Also $0<\\log_2 63<\\log_2 64=6$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)&<\\left(\\frac{\\log_2(7\\cdot9)}{2}\\right)^2\\\\(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)&<\\left(\\frac{\\log_2 63}{2}\\right)^2\\\\(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)&<\\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2\\\\(\\log_2 7)(\\log_2 9)&<9.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 47
      }
    ]
  },
